WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.302 --> 00:00:06.680
MINHAZ KAZI: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.050
Welcome to our session on
building a petabyte scale

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:11.480
reporting pipeline on GCP.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.250
I'm Minhaz Kazi, a developer
advocate at Google,

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.280
and I'll be joined
by Michael Wallman.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.807
Michael is an Infrastructure
Lead at Google Cloud platform.

00:00:20.807 --> 00:00:22.390
In this presentation,
we'll be talking

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.670
about how GCP and
Waze collaborated

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:27.580
to implement a solution for
a petabyte scale reporting

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:28.840
platform.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.580
We'll also show how
this project aligns

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:34.120
with the vision for our
analytics program, the database

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:36.260
management and analytics
vision on the slide.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:40.870
And as you can see, we focus
a lot on an open philosophy.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:42.370
We want to deliver
flexible features

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:44.350
and apply intelligent
Google technologies

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:46.912
to help our customers.

00:00:46.912 --> 00:00:49.120
For the presentation, we'll
be talking about business

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.850
requirements and challenges
that this project had,

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.580
and then we'll also talk about
the technical architectures

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:56.080
and data pipelines.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.520
Finally, we'll talk about
the connector we created

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.470
and then the dashboard
solution we came up with,

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.010
and the outcomes.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:04.989
But first, here's
Tommy Hunt from Waze,

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:07.114
who will be talking about
the business requirements

00:01:07.114 --> 00:01:08.930
and challenges.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:09.970
TOMMY HUNT: Thank you.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:12.880
My name is Tommy Hunt, and
I'm an analyst at Waze.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.770
Many of you may be
familiar with Waze

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:17.170
as a crowdsource navigation
app that helps get you

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:18.730
from place to place.

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:21.610
We have over 130 million
monthly active users

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:25.270
across 185 countries that
collectively completed over one

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.970
billion navigations in 2019.

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:31.150
Our partners and users reported
over 60 million traffic

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:32.860
incidents each
month, and our map

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:35.950
is maintained by 30,000
volunteer map editors.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:41.020
Best of all, Waze is completely
free for anybody to use.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.680
Under the hood, Waze is
even bigger than that.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.250
We're a mobility
platform that helps

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:47.170
people move more freely
in the communities

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:48.730
where they live and work.

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:51.640
Just as we give users access
to real-time information

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.940
within the app, we also partner
with over 1,300 cities, towns,

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.510
and non-profit organizations
to share that data

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.790
through a program we
call Waze for Cities,

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:04.540
helping to inform their mobility
strategies and their responses.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:07.510
In fact, we currently help with
more than 60 crises each year,

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.590
such as hurricanes
and earthquakes,

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:12.010
and we're helping cities
get safer through beacons

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:15.010
by giving drivers a way to stay
connected even when they're out

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.020
of range of GPS.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:19.000
And our newest
initiative, Waze Carpool,

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.910
is helping to combat the
rising congestion in cities

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:25.055
around the world.

00:02:25.055 --> 00:02:27.180
Our city partnerships and
community-driven approach

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.650
is what makes Waze
unique and sets us apart

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:31.010
from other navigation apps.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.010
If you're interested in
learning more about Waze

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.218
or if you're interested in
becoming a Waze for Cities

00:02:35.218 --> 00:02:39.950
partner, visit
Waze.com/WazeForCities.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:41.810
Now, through our Waze
for Cities program,

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:43.940
we offer a variety of
tools and offerings, which

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:48.800
include data sharing, carpool,
beacons, and crisis mitigation.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.500
Today we're going to focus on
how Google Cloud has helped

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.140
us improve one of these
offerings, the data

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.293
that we share with partners.

00:02:57.293 --> 00:02:58.710
Through our data
sharing offering,

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.200
Waze provides real-time
proprietary incident

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.300
and slowdown
information generated

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.210
by millions of Waze drivers
to inform our partners'

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:08.010
mobility strategies.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.530
The data we share includes
user-generated reports

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:13.830
like traffic jams, crashes,
constructions, and potholes,

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.180
just to name a few.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.240
Now before we began
working with Google Cloud,

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.090
Waze data was shared with our
city partners via a GeoRSS feed

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.960
in JSON and XML formats.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:28.390
Partners had to take a lot of
steps to begin using this data.

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:31.590
These steps included ingesting,
storing, and then paying

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:34.110
for the ongoing storage
of this Waze data,

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:36.840
among several other
technical details.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.940
Needless to say, it was
really difficult for many

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:40.680
of our resource-strapped
city partners

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.870
to consider this
process, which really

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:46.735
limited most of our partners'
ability to use our data at all.

00:03:46.735 --> 00:03:48.360
So that's why we
turned to Google Cloud

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.130
to find a way to
manage and share

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:53.730
this data with our partners
in a simpler and overall more

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.432
cost-effective way.

00:03:55.432 --> 00:03:57.390
Now I'll turn it over to
my colleague, Michael,

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:01.837
to talk about the technical
implementation of this tool.

00:04:01.837 --> 00:04:02.670
MICHAEL WALLMAN: OK.

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:03.390
Hi.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:09.330
So, starting with the Waze
and Google Cloud platform

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:13.140
integration, so
obviously BigQuery--

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:17.190
we started with BigQuery in
combination with Data Studio

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:21.900
was the platform or
the services of choice.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:22.400
OK.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:23.250
Challenges.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:29.490
So first, we needed to ingest
data from an outside source.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.970
So Waze provided
us an API to hit,

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:36.750
and we used that API to pull in
all the world's traffic data.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.440
Another challenge
is that the system

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.190
had to support thousands
of partners at once.

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:45.710
So there was no telling when
partners were using the system

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:47.600
or making queries,
so therefore we

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.030
had to assume that there'd
be a lot of concurrency

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:52.100
in the thousands.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:56.930
Simple user-aware dashboards,
meaning that if I'm a partner

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:59.270
and I go to a
dashboard, I would like

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.340
to see the data
that's relevant to me,

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.620
whereas another partner
should see the data that's

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.700
relevant to them without
moving around or having

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:10.250
to set that on every login.

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:11.510
So that was really important.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:14.240
Another challenge would be,
if I were to make changes

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.700
to the default
dashboards, they should

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:18.170
propagate for all partners.

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:22.640
So I didn't want to have to edit
or have individual dashboards

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:24.380
per partner.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:27.060
It was a requester
does not pay model,

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:30.140
meaning that partners are
not charged for this service,

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:33.950
and that Google or
Waze, in this case

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:36.350
is providing the
service for free.

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:38.600
And then one of the
major challenges,

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:41.450
which wasn't technical, is that
it was a small development team

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:43.140
with little to no SRE support.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:46.630
OK.

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:49.300
Before I go into details on
the technical architecture,

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.730
I want to call out a
teammate, Egan [? Suarez, ?]

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:53.380
from our lab tech
customer engineering

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:57.640
team, who really helped design
and develop these pipelines.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:00.850
I myself worked with Egan,
but also mainly focused

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:05.580
on the connector and the
dashboard development.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:09.570
So number one, we had
our data pipelines.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:13.570
So we ingested the world's
traffic data, in essence,

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:15.150
into BigQuery.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:16.680
Number two, user management.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:21.090
Partners onboarded to the system
through a simple web portal.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:24.330
And then number three, they
go to a default dashboard.

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:27.330
They get access to the data
that's of interest to them.

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:32.150
And number four is how to
use the partner's behavior,

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:35.700
or how the partners are
accessing the data, to make

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.850
judgments on what data is
valuable to our partners

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:41.580
and what dashboards are
they really finding value

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:45.880
in so we can modify the
dashboards accordingly.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:46.380
OK.

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:48.420
The data pipeline.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:52.830
We used App Engine Flex and
its Chrome functionality

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:57.810
to stream data in from
Waze's traffic API

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:00.510
into BigQuery partition tables.

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:02.890
And so Cron would
run periodically,

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:05.280
and we would basically
query that API

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:09.050
and pull in all the data for
that period since the last run,

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.340
and write it into
partition tables.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:14.280
And then, from these
partition tables,

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:17.340
we set up BigQuery
scheduled jobs.

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.640
And so what they would do is
they would transform and dedupe

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.915
the data writing into
cluster tables in BigQuery.

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:28.490
OK.

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:31.100
User management.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:35.330
The user management
consisted of partners

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:40.000
logging into a web portal
and selecting the polygons

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.640
they were interested in.

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:44.650
And this gets written into
a partner table in BigQuery.

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:48.760
We chose to use BigQuery
because, just for simplicity--

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:50.890
this is a very small
table so it doesn't really

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:53.890
fit the BigQuery use case,
but for simplicity we

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:57.190
just decided to keep the
user information in BigQuery

00:07:57.190 --> 00:07:58.450
as well.

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:03.640
So following a similar
pattern as the data pipelines,

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:09.190
we have an App Engine job that
uses Cron to run periodically.

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:12.070
It looks at the partner
table, and based

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:17.260
on changes, whether a partner
gets added or removed,

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:21.820
it creates authorized views
for these partners in BigQuery.

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:25.870
Now as you see from
the diagram, there's

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:27.970
something called
intermediate views.

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:31.950
And we had to add
these to work around

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:36.370
the 2,500 limit of
authorized use per data set.

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:40.450
So, in essence, we have
created views of views.

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:45.490
And intermediate views
basically use an ASCII regex

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:49.480
to [? doc-route ?] partners in
different intermediate views.

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:54.090
And this allowed us to
get over that 2,500 limit.

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:56.640
And now Minhaz is this
going to cover the reporting

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.062
requirements.

00:08:59.062 --> 00:09:00.395
MINHAZ KAZI: Thank you, Michael.

00:09:00.395 --> 00:09:04.400
So stemming from the
technical challenges we had,

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:07.520
we determined what the
requirements for the reporting

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:08.180
would be.

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:10.430
First we'll set the
data for separate cities

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:12.060
were in separate data sets.

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:15.000
It would have to be accessed
from separate data sets.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.390
And there we'd have
to ensure data privacy

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:20.030
because we didn't want
any of the partners

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:22.110
to see other partner's data.

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:24.432
So if they were looking
at a dashboard or any kind

00:09:24.432 --> 00:09:26.240
of reporting solution,
they would only

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:29.703
be able to see their own city's
data and not anyone else's one.

00:09:29.703 --> 00:09:32.120
There was also the requirement
for the centralized billing

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:36.200
to have that frictionless user
experience because, since we

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:39.830
didn't want the city's
partners to carry

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:42.020
the bill for this
dashboard, the billing would

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:45.408
be managed centrally by
the Waze and GCP team.

00:09:45.408 --> 00:09:47.200
So there would be a
central billing account

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:50.030
that would be taking care
of the BigQuery querying

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:51.493
through the dashboard.

00:09:51.493 --> 00:09:53.660
There would also have to
be a single point of change

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:55.760
for all the dashboard edits.

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:58.460
That means if a future change
was made to the dashboard

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:00.680
or if any kind of
update was to be made,

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:02.280
it would have to get
rolled out to all

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:03.990
the partners at the same time.

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:06.440
So there would have to be some
kind of an easy deployment

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:07.950
system for that.

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:12.070
And based on these requirements,
we decided to use Data Studio.

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:15.920
Data Studio is a data
visualization and reporting

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:19.370
tool that's available freely
from Google Marketing platform.

00:10:19.370 --> 00:10:21.380
And it works very
well with BigQuery.

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:23.360
It has a native
connector to BigQuery.

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:25.820
If you're using BigQuery
data in Data Studio

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:28.220
you can use the PI
engine to take advantage

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:30.470
of its fast in-memory
calculations

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:33.800
and get very speedy results.

00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:37.220
So as the team was looking
into this solution,

00:10:37.220 --> 00:10:40.700
we realized that there were
several alternatives available.

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:42.290
First was using the
native connector

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:45.730
to create a single dashboard
with viewer screenshots.

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:48.020
What viewer screenshots
mean is that when someone

00:10:48.020 --> 00:10:50.300
would view the dashboard,
their credentials

00:10:50.300 --> 00:10:52.310
would get passed
through Data Studio

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:55.400
into the data source, which
here would be BigQuery.

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:57.530
And then that
credentials would be

00:10:57.530 --> 00:11:01.580
used to enforce what data
they would get to see.

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:06.230
And here would be using
role level access,

00:11:06.230 --> 00:11:09.950
either through Date Studio's
email-based filtering

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:12.713
or BigQuery's [INAUDIBLE]
user function.

00:11:12.713 --> 00:11:14.630
But the limitation here
was that, if we wanted

00:11:14.630 --> 00:11:16.130
to use either of
those two features,

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:18.200
we'd have to march
all those data

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:21.260
sets into one single
table or source

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:22.763
and then filter
from there, which

00:11:22.763 --> 00:11:24.680
became a little bit
complicated, because there

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:27.740
were over 1,400 partners.

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:30.800
The second option was to use,
again, the native connector,

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:33.200
but there we would create
one template dashboard

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:37.130
and then copy over the
dashboard multiple times,

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:39.020
one time for each
partner, and then

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:42.595
edit the data source so that it
would point to the right data

00:11:42.595 --> 00:11:43.640
set.

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:45.920
That had its own
challenges because it

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:47.990
would be possible to
copy over the dashboards,

00:11:47.990 --> 00:11:50.300
but if we wanted to
make any future changes

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:53.570
or wanted to do maintenance,
doing it over 1,400

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:56.940
different dashboards would
become near impossible.

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:58.790
So we opted for the
third solution, which

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:00.440
was the custom
connector, and we'll

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:02.750
go into that in
more details later.

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:04.700
But what the custom
connector would do

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:07.970
is it would make a
customized solution involving

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:09.400
Data Studio and BigQuery.

00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:11.480
It would meet all the
reporting requirements

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:13.850
that I just mentioned
earlier, and this

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:16.100
would record any minimum
level of development

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:19.070
from the team that
was working on it.

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:23.340
The custom connectors are built
on the Community Connector

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:24.930
platform for Data Studio.

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:27.000
These are written
in App Script, which

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:32.250
is Google's cloud-based
serverless scripting engine.

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:35.310
And it takes a very
low amount of effort

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:38.520
to build connectors
using this framework.

00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:40.050
Anyone can build a connector.

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:42.270
You can connect to any
external data sources

00:12:42.270 --> 00:12:44.270
and pull in the data
into a Data Studio.

00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:47.340
And it's also possible to share
and publish those connectors

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:48.900
so that others can use.

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:50.580
If you are interested
in learning more

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:52.500
about these connectors,
you can check out

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:57.900
the Codelab we have at
this URL on the screen.

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.490
The Community Connector is
already an established program

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:03.370
where there are over 200
published connectors available.

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:06.370
And here at Google we ourselves
have used this platform

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:11.140
to create several connectors
to serve data to the public.

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:13.450
For example, the
ChromeUX Connector

00:13:13.450 --> 00:13:17.260
uses this connector platform
to serve ChromeUX report data

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:19.130
to users.

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:21.560
So it was already a
proven solution for us.

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:24.940
However, one thing we have
never tried with these solutions

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:28.630
was that we didn't implement
the user-based view

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:30.940
in any of these connectors.

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:33.490
So what that means is,
in user-based view,

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:35.750
multiple users will look
at the same dashboard,

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:38.260
but based on their
credentials, who they are,

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:40.780
it would fetch data from
different data sets, which

00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:43.190
is a bit different from
roll over access, because we

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:45.460
are accessing completely
different data sets.

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:47.980
And then the dashboard would
get populated with data

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:49.420
from those data sets.

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:51.160
So this was something
new that the team

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:56.170
was trying out with the
Community Connector approach.

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:58.210
And our end result
would look something

00:13:58.210 --> 00:14:00.850
similar to this, that
the connector would

00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:03.540
get created, and using
that connector we'd create

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:05.680
a dashboard, a single
dashboard link, and that

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:08.440
would be sent to all the
1,400 different partners.

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:10.940
When a partner opens
their dashboard link,

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:13.410
they will see their dashboard
with their own data,

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:15.550
and they wouldn't have
any flexibility or option

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:18.860
to switch over to see a
separate user's dashboard.

00:14:18.860 --> 00:14:21.280
So this is a complete user
experience we wanted to have.

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:24.110
A user gets a
link, clicks on it,

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:26.210
they immediately
view their report.

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:28.300
So to do this under
the hood, this

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:29.680
is what we had to implement.

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:31.720
So once the user clicks
on the dashboard link,

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:34.090
it opens up the Data
Studio dashboard.

00:14:34.090 --> 00:14:38.260
And Data Studio will then
execute the connector

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:40.690
with viewer screenshots.

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:43.317
The first time any user
opens this dashboard,

00:14:43.317 --> 00:14:45.400
they will have to go through
an authorization flow

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:47.410
where they authorize
this connector

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:48.910
to run on their behalf.

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:51.880
But this is done only for
the first time they use it.

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:53.950
For all subsequent
times, the dashboard

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:57.055
will open up automatically.

00:14:57.055 --> 00:14:58.930
So Data Studio executes
the connector script,

00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:01.810
and the connector script then
takes the user's credentials

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:03.370
and figures out,
all right, this is

00:15:03.370 --> 00:15:06.310
the email for this user
who's viewing the dashboard.

00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:09.580
And then it makes a call
to the BigQuery REST API

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:14.600
to figure out which data set
should this user be viewing.

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:17.710
So we had a mapping table
that mapped each user's email

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:20.650
address with a specific
list of data sets.

00:15:20.650 --> 00:15:24.340
So this REST API call enabled
us to query into BigQuery

00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:26.560
and get the result
back where we would

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:29.350
say, all right, this
email address, x, has

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:31.170
access to data set y.

00:15:31.170 --> 00:15:34.120
So once this information
was back in the connector,

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:36.910
we'd then construct a new query.

00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:38.770
And this query
string would contain,

00:15:38.770 --> 00:15:42.690
basically this data set
idea that we just fetched.

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:45.130
So it would say something
like, select [? App Script ?]

00:15:45.130 --> 00:15:46.913
from data set y.

00:15:46.913 --> 00:15:49.330
But we would not send this
query off to BigQuery directly.

00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:51.310
What we would do is
that then the connector

00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:55.120
would use the Data
Studio at-home services

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:57.590
to make the query
directly into BigQuery.

00:15:57.590 --> 00:16:01.120
So let's then look a bit deeper
into that, what that means.

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:03.640
To communicate
between Data Studio

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:05.840
and BigQuery the native
connector directly works.

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:08.440
But when you are working
with a community connector,

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:11.630
the data will usually have
to pass through App Script,

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:13.630
and it will have to be
transformed and processed

00:16:13.630 --> 00:16:17.660
in App Script which
adds a bit of overhead

00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:19.750
in calculation and latency.

00:16:19.750 --> 00:16:23.170
Using Advance Services for
Data Studio, what we can do is,

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:25.600
rather than passing the
data through App Script,

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:28.660
we can just pass the query
itself to Data Studio,

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:32.320
and the Data Studio will pass
that query to BigQuery directly

00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:35.680
and fetch the data from BigQuery
using a native connection.

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:38.770
The data does not pass
through App Script in any way,

00:16:38.770 --> 00:16:41.890
so it results in a much
faster interaction.

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:44.020
All the features are
ultimately pushed down,

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:47.380
and then the users also get a
much better, faster dashboard

00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:49.410
experience.

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:51.870
So finally, Data Studio
passes that query

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:55.750
into BigQuery, which is the
final query for the dashboard,

00:16:55.750 --> 00:17:00.450
which contains only the data
set that this user has access

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:01.140
to see.

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:03.780
And finally, the results
are sent back to Data Studio

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:07.069
and Data Studio renders
that data in dashboard,

00:17:07.069 --> 00:17:08.990
and the viewer finally
can see their dashboard

00:17:08.990 --> 00:17:10.339
with their own data.

00:17:10.339 --> 00:17:11.990
So since in the
return queries results

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:15.130
step, only the user's
data set is passed,

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:18.780
there is no way for a different
user to see that data set

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:22.680
or for this user to see data
from a different data set.

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:24.652
So what does the end
result look like?

00:17:24.652 --> 00:17:26.819
This is the whole process,
but when the user finally

00:17:26.819 --> 00:17:28.950
opens the dashboard,
they get a view

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:30.430
that looks something like this.

00:17:30.430 --> 00:17:32.730
This is the view that all
the users-- all the 1,400

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:33.730
different partner-- see.

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:36.742
When they open the dashboard,
they get a dashboard like this.

00:17:36.742 --> 00:17:37.950
At the top there are filters.

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:39.510
They can change the filters.

00:17:39.510 --> 00:17:41.400
They can interact with
it and this dashboard

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:44.310
will interactively present
the data for whatever features

00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:46.680
or selections they're making.

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:49.590
Each partner would
see only their view

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:52.710
and they wouldn't
see anything else.

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:54.570
And based on this
dashboard, the team

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:56.460
made some further
improvements where

00:17:56.460 --> 00:17:58.020
they added for their pages.

00:17:58.020 --> 00:18:00.750
They also added some
monitoring where

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:03.420
they could measure how the
dashboard was being used

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:07.020
through Stackdriver, which
partners were using it,

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:11.350
which partners were incurring
additional data costs.

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:15.910
It's also possible to set
up data limits by partner.

00:18:15.910 --> 00:18:18.720
So a feature would
potentially work

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:21.900
is where we could potentially
take this solution

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:25.797
and then build in limits where
each partner would get, say,

00:18:25.797 --> 00:18:26.880
a certain amount of limit.

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:28.450
If they hit that
limit every month,

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:31.520
then their dashboard
wouldn't become visible.

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:33.060
Another potential
solution that can

00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:34.980
be implemented on
top of this is,

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:37.410
using Google Analytics
for Data Studio,

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:39.210
it can be measured
how the users are

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:40.540
interacting with the dashboard.

00:18:40.540 --> 00:18:42.150
Which pages are they looking at?

00:18:42.150 --> 00:18:45.400
Which places are they
spending the most time on?

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:49.050
And then make the dashboard
much better and faster and more

00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:52.100
usable for users that way.

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:55.550
If anyone wants to build
a similar solution using

00:18:55.550 --> 00:18:59.332
Data Studio, Advance
Services, and BigQuery,

00:18:59.332 --> 00:19:00.540
these links would be helpful.

00:19:00.540 --> 00:19:03.470
The first link basically
provides a step

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:06.750
by step guide on how to build
a connector similar to this.

00:19:06.750 --> 00:19:08.900
And the second link
contains a code example

00:19:08.900 --> 00:19:10.003
of a similar connector.

00:19:10.003 --> 00:19:11.420
You can just take
the code example

00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:15.370
and customize it
to your own needs.

00:19:15.370 --> 00:19:17.520
So what finally happened
after this project?

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:20.910
This project was
launched in October 2019.

00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:23.610
After launch, there were
numerous positive news stories.

00:19:23.610 --> 00:19:26.850
Over 200 news stories were
published on Digital Trends

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:28.110
and Venture Beats.

00:19:28.110 --> 00:19:31.800
Right now over 6,000 users
from more than 1,400 partners

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:35.250
across the world are using
this dashboard every day.

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:37.400
And as Michael
mentioned earlier,

00:19:37.400 --> 00:19:39.900
the connector that he created
and the dashboard that he came

00:19:39.900 --> 00:19:43.710
up with, it's working basically
in a [? no-ops ?] fashion,

00:19:43.710 --> 00:19:45.750
even with such high
amount of usage.

00:19:45.750 --> 00:19:48.420
And there are over 30,000
accesses of the Data Studio

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:50.880
dashboard, and more
than 800,000 queries

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:55.070
have been made in BigQuery
by all these partners.

00:19:55.070 --> 00:19:57.850
Now this was a
major collaboration

00:19:57.850 --> 00:20:00.020
between Waze and
Google Cloud, and Waze

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:02.080
plans to continue to
promote the data tools

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:04.510
and build education
materials to aid traffic

00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:06.230
planning across the world.

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:08.750
And that brings us to the
end of our presentation.

00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:10.380
Thank you.

